.TH XZ 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
xz, unxz, xzcat \- decompress .xz files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xz
.RB { \-d | \-t }
.RB [ \-cfkqv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B unxz
.RB [ \-cdfkqtv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B xzcat
.RB [ \-cdfkqtv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xz
decompresses files in the
.B .xz
format of XZ Utils.
It cannot compress.
.B unxz
is
.BR "xz \-d" ,
and
.B xzcat
is
.BR "xz \-dc" .
.PP
Each
.I file
must end in
.BR .xz ,
which is removed to name the output file in the same directory, so
.I notes.txt.xz
decompresses to
.IR notes.txt .
On success the input file is deleted, unless
.B \-k
is given.
A file with any other suffix is skipped with an error.
The output file must not already exist, unless
.B \-f
is given.
It is read-only if the input file is, and its modification time is the
time of decompression.
.PP
With
.BR \-c ,
or as
.BR xzcat ,
the data of each
.I file
is written in turn to standard output, the input files are kept, and
their names need no suffix.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
.B xz
reads standard input and writes standard output.
When standard input is a console,
.B xz
exits with an error unless
.B \-f
is given.
.PP
An input may hold several concatenated
.B .xz
streams, which are decoded in sequence.
A file that cannot be opened, has the wrong suffix, has an existing
output file, or fails to decode is reported on standard error, and the
remaining files are still processed.
When decoding fails, the partial output file is deleted and the input
file is kept.
With
.BR \-c ,
data decoded before the failure has already been written.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-dk ,
and may follow the
.I file
operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B \-d
Decompress.
.B xz
requires
.B \-d
or
.BR \-t ,
and
.B unxz
and
.B xzcat
ignore it.
.TP
.B \-c
Write to standard output and keep the input files.
.TP
.B \-f
Replace an existing output file, even a read-only one, and allow reading
compressed data from a console.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep the input files instead of deleting them.
.TP
.B \-t
Test: decompress each
.I file
and discard the output.
Input files are kept, and their names need no suffix.
See
.B NOTES
for the damage a test cannot detect.
.TP
.B \-q
Accepted and ignored.
.TP
.B \-v
After decompressing a file to a new file, print a line such as
.B "notes.txt.xz: 71% \- replaced with notes.txt"
on standard error, even with
.BR \-k .
The percentage is the size reduction relative to the decompressed size,
rounded up.
When the
.B .xz
file is larger than its contents, it wraps around to a huge number.
Nothing is printed with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-t ,
or for standard input.
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments, such as
.BR *.xz ,
are expanded at startup, for use from
.BR cmd.exe .
When it is unset, it is set to
.B 0
for child processes, so the kit's shell, which expands wildcards itself,
turns this off for the commands it runs.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
An octal file mode creation mask for output files.
Only the owner write bit has an effect: a mask such as
.B 222
creates output files read-only.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, options must come before the
.I file
operands, and a later argument that begins with
.B \-
is a file name.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every file was decompressed or tested.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option,
.B xz
without
.B \-d
or
.BR \-t ,
or an error with any input.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decompress a file, replacing it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ unxz notes.txt.xz
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decompress a file and keep the original:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ unxz \-k notes.txt.xz
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract a source tarball:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ xzcat project\-1.0.tar.xz | tar xf \-
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check that downloaded archives decode:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ xz \-t *.tar.xz
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
This
.B xz
is the busybox decoder, based on XZ Embedded.
Unlike XZ Utils, it verifies only CRC32 integrity checks.
CRC64 checks, the XZ Utils default, and SHA-256 checks are skipped
without a warning, so
.B \-t
detects damage to such a file only where it breaks decoding.
.PP
Only LZMA2 data without other filters decodes.
A file made with a BCJ or delta filter, such as with the XZ Utils option
.BR \-\-x86 ,
fails as corrupted data, as does one with a dictionary larger than
64 MiB.
The XZ Utils presets, including
.BR \-9 ,
use at most 64 MiB.
Every decoding failure is reported as
.BR "corrupted data" ,
without the file name.
.PP
Where a stream may begin, zero bytes are skipped, and any byte other
than
.BR 0xFD ,
the first byte of the
.B .xz
signature, ends decoding without error.
So data after the last stream is ignored unless its first nonzero byte
is
.BR 0xFD .
Input whose first nonzero byte is not
.BR 0xFD ,
including empty input, decodes to nothing and succeeds.
As a result,
.B unxz
replaces a file with the
.B .xz
suffix that is empty or holds another format, such as gzip, with an
empty file.
Zero bytes at the end of the input, which the format allows as stream
padding, are reported as corrupted data.
.PP
Only the suffix
.B .xz
is recognized, not
.BR .txz ,
and it must be lowercase, though Windows file names are otherwise not
case sensitive.
Use
.B \-c
to decompress files with other names.
A read-only input file is deleted after decompression like any other.
.PP
A
.B \-
operand after a
.I file
operand does not read standard input.
With
.B \-c
or
.BR \-t ,
it decodes nothing and succeeds.
Otherwise it fails with
.BR "read error" ,
and
.B xz
exits without processing the
.I file
operands after it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR bzip2 (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR lzma (1),
.BR tar (1),
.BR zstd (1)
